  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 226 PM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nAs the cold front passes through along with the upper trough passing \nthe Rockies, the Northern Plains including our area will get a jolt \nof colder temperatures heading into the weekend and continuing \nthrough early next week. Daytime highs are generally only expected \nto be in the 30s to 40s, which will be about 5 to 10 degrees below \nnormal for this time of year as well as overnight lows being in the \nteens to 20s each night. \n\nAttention next turns to early next week for the next potential \nimpacting storm system. A split upper flow is expected with a \nsouthern stream over southern CONUS, with the northern stream is \nover northern CONUS. The northern stream is what could impact the \nNorthern Plains as mostly zonal flow aloft will make way for a \ndigging trough moving across the Rockies Monday night into Tuesday \nmorning. Current guidance suggests a weak frontal boundary \ndeveloping ahead of the trough Tuesday morning into afternoon across \nthe Panhandle where a tight band of FGEN develops. This could \nprovide enough lift for a tight band of snow to fall across the \neastern Panhandle to portions of north central Nebraska. Current \nthinking is there being at least a 25 percent chance per NBM of at \nleast a inch of snow across north central Nebraska by Tuesday \nnight. There remains however quite a bit of uncertainty with \nexactly where the FGEN band will line up which will greatly \nimpact exact locations for snow totals at this time. Further \nforecast packages will have a better grasp of the environment as\ndays progress into the holiday travel time.",
